Writer: importing a .docx with thousands of tables no longer scans every table for each new one.

SwDoc::GetUniqueTableName() scanned all table formats and asked each whether it is used (SwDoc::IsUsed
broadcasts to the table, SwTable::SwClientNotify -> FindTableNode) every time a table was created, so
the import of T tables cost O(T^2). In a synthetic document of 4000 small tables 6 of 14 stack samples of
the import were there; a 10 MB text document with 8800 tables spent an estimated 60 s on it (Linux) and
the import on a 4-core Windows PC showed "Not responding" for 140 s.

During a writerfilter import (SwDoc::IsInWriterfilterImport) the result of the previous call is kept
(SwDoc::TableNameCache): when the table format list only grew by one format at its end, only that
format is checked; any other change (a removed or reordered format, SetTableName, the end of the
import) falls back to the full scan, which rebuilds the cache. The returned name is unchanged: the
smallest n >= 1 for which no used, non-default format is called "Table<n>", at most count + 1.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).
Test: CppunitTest_sw_writerfilter_ooxml testTableNamesDuringImport; the table names of the upstream
.docx test documents are identical with and without the patch; tests/unit/test_large_office_open_patches.py.
